00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.180
Some bacon, some eggs and toast with butter.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.060
Oh, my goodness, turkey bacon, I'm sure, right?

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.060
No, sir.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.460
Unfortunately, the full thing, OK.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:25.660
Very, very good.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.660
Oh yeah.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:32.512
So I wanted to ask I actually had a conversation
with a really good friend of mine that I've

00:00:32.512 --> 00:00:34.210
had since early childhood.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:39.129
And last night I had a conversation with him
about politics and we've never talked about

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:40.129
it before.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:43.139
I consume a lot of political content.

00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:50.510
And, you know, he I think this kind of ties
into the whole, like, deprogramming thing,

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:56.699
so I was talking to him and he he thoroughly
believes all the talking points, but he doesn't

00:00:56.699 --> 00:00:58.859
consume any media.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:00:59.929
I asked him about it.

00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:01.969
He doesn't really watch the media.

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:05.580
He doesn't really read any news.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.110
He knows all this stuff through word of mouth,
which is kind of scary.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.050
Now, when you say word of mouth, you mean
like literally do you mean people at work

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:17.320
telling him or are you including social media
and word of mouth?

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.080
Um, I think social media actually might play
a role.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.080
Right.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:28.600
But, yeah, I was that's that's I think the
key thing, because at this point, you know,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:34.010
when we talk about like Fox News, for example,
only a few million people are ever watching

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.730
any particular episode of Fox News.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.910
This country has three hundred and thirty
one million people.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:42.350
But the point is Fox News has agenda setting
power.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:47.280
And then you see the stories repeated on Facebook
and on Twitter, et cetera.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.820
And that's where it's being.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:49.970
That's where it's being repeated.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.110
And that makes sense.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.480
Yeah.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:58.060
And so so we were going back and forth about
the election fraud thing, and I kept trying

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:02.490
to I don't think he really understood, you
know, how all this stuff works.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.240
And I think, you know, I think a lot of people
don't.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.240
Yeah.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.240
Like what?

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.240
What do you think?

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.280
And it was it was so hard to, you know, be
polite.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.280
Right.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.319
Because as someone who's paying attention
to this stuff, it's like it's it's it's really

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:14.760
frustrating, you know?

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.859
So I guess what I just wanted to pick your
brain and see, like, what do you think maybe

00:02:17.859 --> 00:02:20.689
some strategies might be like?

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:24.700
I guess, like Comley, you know, attack this.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.700
I guess.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.370
I mean, with someone that's that uninformed
and who's basically just getting what other

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.560
people they know are sharing on Facebook,
for example, they're not even necessarily

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.700
it's not even about getting them to look at
different news outlets because they're not

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.529
even looking at news.

00:02:39.529 --> 00:02:46.709
You have to start really softly with where
where did you where did you learn that?

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:50.739
And getting them to say and then say, oh,
and how do you know that that's trustworthy?

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:54.349
You know, they say, well, my aunt, Aunt Beatrice,
shared it on Facebook.

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:57.640
OK, well, what what does Aunt Beatrice do
for work?

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.150
Oh, well, I don't know.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.620
She works in a factory.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:01.620
All right.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.909
So clearly she got it from somewhere.

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:04.599
She this wasn't her research.

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:05.599
Where did she get it?

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:07.540
Well, I guess I don't know where she got it.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.680
OK, well, you know, you can you can find out
for yourself.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.859
There are primary sources on this stuff.

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:17.829
You've got to start getting them open to the
idea that they may be hearing things that

00:03:17.829 --> 00:03:18.920
aren't true.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.150
But it is it's really tough.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.670
It's very, very difficult.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:26.360
Yeah, it can be really hard when, like one
thing I think a lot of people in the right

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:32.099
tend to do is like they'll do all their own
research and they'll end up in a lot of very

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:33.099
interesting conclusions.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:34.099
Right.

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:38.189
But research often just means you look at
articles on Breitbart, you know.

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:39.279
Yeah, pretty much.

00:03:39.279 --> 00:03:40.279
Right.

00:03:40.279 --> 00:03:43.140
And then it's like, well, I can come back
at them with ten articles that say, hey, you're

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.140
not right, man.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.499
Yeah, well, you can't believe everything you
read on the Internet.

00:03:46.499 --> 00:03:48.790
And it's just like, oh man, this.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:50.260
Yeah, it can be a little stressful.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.010
I mean, the problem is that with a lot of
these things, you know, there's this false

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:58.480
notion that it's up to sort of like opinion,
like, for example, just cutting taxes for

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.639
the rich, help the economy.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:02.040
That's an empirical question.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.809
And we can look in history at changes to tax
rates and then the associated changes in GDP,

00:04:07.809 --> 00:04:09.360
employment, et cetera.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.309
There's a lot of people who, unfortunately,
are in the paradigm of, well, I don't know,

00:04:13.309 --> 00:04:17.311
because, you know, the nation says one thing
and Breitbart says a different thing.

00:04:17.311 --> 00:04:21.070
And then Tucker says one thing, but then MSNBC
says something else.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:28.820
You can go and look at economic papers that
are peer reviewed from, you know, groups of

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.870
economists and actually answered that question.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:38.260
But the problem is, a lot of people either
aren't aware or don't know or have the interest

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:39.510
in actually doing that.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:42.820
But a lot of these things are really they're
knowable, they're not really opinion.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.260
They're not really up for debate.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:52.591
Yeah, so I guess I guess like a big first
step is going to be trying to, I guess, get

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:54.540
like.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.600
Get these people to understand that academics
have what is it like?

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.420
Like they're held accountable, it has accountability.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:06.470
Yeah, and I think the first step is just getting
people to recognize that there's limits to

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.470
what they know.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:12.530
If once people recognize that their their
knowledge is limited and there's more out

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:13.790
there, that's step one.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:18.040
And unfortunately, a lot of people are either
just naturally incurious or are just busy

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.160
living their lives where they take that thing
they see on Facebook.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:22.340
And that's as far as they go.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.450
And that's that's that's not a good recipe.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:28.550
Yeah, that makes Kenny great to hear from
you.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:29.740
I really appreciate it.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:30.740
Thank you.

